    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Brings Fresh Adventure-Ready Concepts at the 2026 SEMA Show Under the Woodland Banner\nMercedes-Benz Delays GLC Production in the US From November 2027 to May 2030\n33 photos\nPhoto: Mercedes-AMG\nHistorically speaking, the GLE midsizer and the more compact GLC are the most popular Mercedes nameplates in the United States of America. The German manufacturer also has an American factory that needs more work, but alas, there is a tariff bill that won't disappear anytime soon.\nMercedes-Benz AG has confirmed that it's delaying GLC production at the Alabama complex from November 2027 to May 2030, leaving the GLC dependent on German production for another two and a half years. This information comes from Automotive News, which underlines that GLC accounts for a quarter of the brand's passenger vehicle sales in the US market.\nAlmost 54,000 units were sold between January 2026 and September 2026, more than the next three most popular imported Mercs combined. Every one of those German-built models faces a 15% import tariff stateside, eating into their profit margins. Of equal significance, that's a rather expensive way to supply a lucrative market where the GLC already does so well.\nAutomotive News cites two people familiar with the company's plans, yet a Mercedes spokesperson declined to comment on the revised schedule. What is clear, though, is that Mercedes-Benz AG committed $4 billion to its US manufacturing operations. A key part of that investment is GLC localization, and the delay comes down to costs associated with the generational switchover.\nMore specifically, the automaker from Stuttgart apparently decided against moving the current GLC to the Vance, Alabama, production facility in favor of waiting for the next generation. Internally referred to as V255, the newcomer should arrive by 2030 because the outgoing 254 series rolled out in 2022.\nPhoto: Mercedes-AMG Just like the 254, the 255 series is expected with a selection of four- and six-cylinder lumps, along with a choice of plug-in hybrid powertrains. Whether the German automaker will also make a case for V8 muscle, that remains to be seen.\nSpeaking to the cited publication, Sam Fiorani of AutoForecast Solutions claims that prepping a production complex for a model that is approaching the end of its lifecycle would require another expensive changeover when the next gen drops. Given that every automaker out there is aggressively saving money however possible, Merc's decision makes plenty of sense from a financial standpoint.\nThink of it as very sensible planning. As for the import tariff, the situation becomes considerably less attractive for Mercedes-Benz AG. The company will continue importing the GLC from Germany while it waits for the V255. The 15% duty applies to all imported vehicles, giving Mercedes another cost to absorb. And those costs will definitely make a dent in the automaker's financial statements.\nConsidering that Mercedes hasn't even commented on this purported GLC US production delay, Mercedes has not disclosed how much the delay could add to its tariff bill either. Similarly, we don't know whether Merc intends to offset the expense through higher prices, lower margins, or production efficiencies.\nAlso known as MBUSI, the Alabama plant currently makes the GLE and GLS lines of sport utility vehicles. The EQE SUV and EQS SUV are made there as well.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMercedes-Benz Mercedes-Benz GLC Mercedes-AMG GLC Mercedes-AMG production US\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Volkswagen Puts a Price Tag on the 2027 California, Here’s How Much the Campervan Costs",
